## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP with a constrained path problem and reconstruction, a common real interview topic for large-scale environments like PayPal or Cloudflare.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP state: i, j, t, dir (0 right, 1 down)
- Turn counting via transition from one dir to the other
- Base cases for first row/column
- Path reconstruction pointers and memory/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])
    )
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) dp[0][j][0][0] = dp[0][j-1][0][0] + grid[0][j];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) dp[i][0][0][1] = dp[i-1][0][0][1] + grid[i][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (j > 0) {
          const fromLeftSame = dp[i][j-1][t][0];
          const fromLeftTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][0], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromLeftSame, fromLeftTurn));
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          const fromTopSame = dp[i-1][j][t][1];
          const fromTopTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][1], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromTopSame, fromTopTurn));
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = NEG, bestT = 0, bestDir = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
      if (dp[n-1][m-1][t][d] > best) {
        best = dp[n-1][m-1][t][d]; bestT = t; bestDir = d;
      }
    }
  }
  return {best, bestT, bestDir};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the path coordinates efficiently?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n*m*k) or O(k) with rolling arrays?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests a standard DP with an adjacency constraint and a limit on picks, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i and t with i-2 due to adjacency
- Reconstruction via a take/parent trace
- Space/time trade-offs; potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonAdjSum(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i >= 2 && dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct indices
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      indices.push(i-1);
      i -= 2;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  return { maxProfit: dp[n][k], indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP formulation for selecting multiple non-overlapping subarrays, a common beginner challenge with practical reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t]: best sum using first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- end[i][t]: best subarray sum ending at i-1 for t subarrays
- Transitions combine extending a subarray or starting a new one
- Reconstruction via backtracking boundaries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most-k subarrays?
- How would you retrieve the actual subarray boundaries efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a less-traveled DP angle: maximizing a sum of subarray-specific statistics (distinct counts) with exact segmentation. Requires careful state design and backtracking for reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix end i and segment count t
- Subarray distinct counts D(p,i-1)
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Time: O(n^2 k), Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDistinctPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  // Precompute distinct counts D[l][r]
  const D = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      seen.add(A[r]);
      D[l][r] = seen.size;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const bt = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = D[0][i - 1];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 1; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const cand = dp[p][t - 1] + D[p][i - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; bt[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i = n, t = k;
  const segs = [];
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = bt[i][t];
    segs.push([p, i - 1]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  segs.reverse();
  return { best: dp[n][k], segments: segs };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) memory?
- How would you handle at most k segments instead of exactly k?
- Can you adapt to streaming data or reconstruct segments efficiently for large n?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests deriving a solvable DP for contiguous partitions with a length-based cost, plus a linear-time optimization and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Contiguous partition DP
- Prefix sums and O(nk) optimization
- Backtracking to report segment endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxTotalWithCost(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    let bestVal = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const j = i - 1;
      if (dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] - S[j] + c * j;
        if (cand > bestVal) { bestVal = cand; bestIdx = j; }
      }
      if (bestVal > -Infinity) {
        dp[i][t] = S[i] - c * i + c + bestVal;
        par[i][t] = bestIdx;
      }
    }
  }
  const segments = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  if (dp[n][k] <= -Infinity/2) return {best: -Infinity, segments: []};
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    segments.push([j+1, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  segments.reverse();
  return {best: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if c is paid only for non-first element or per segment boundary?
- How would you extend to maximize total value for at most k segments instead of exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design for selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with reconstruction. Requires prefix sums, state definition, and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP over positions and count of subarrays
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Reconstructing intervals with predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs (O(nk) vs optimizations)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length; const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const from = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>new Array(k+1).fill(null));
  for (let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    let best = -Infinity;
    let bestIdx = -1;
    for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      if (dp[i-1][t-1] - P[i-1] > best){ best = dp[i-1][t-1] - P[i-1]; bestIdx = i-1; }
      const take = P[i] + best;
      if (take > dp[i-1][t]){ dp[i][t] = take; from[i][t] = [bestIdx+1, i]; }
      else { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction (omitted for brevity in this snippet)
  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], intervals: []};
}
```

